 [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.98].\n\n### 1. Definitions and Calculation Methods\n\n* **GAAP (IFRS) EPS:** Calculated directly from the consolidated financial statements by dividing the reported net earnings (or loss) after tax by the weighted average number of common shares outstanding (basic and diluted) [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.4, p.98]. It includes all accounting charges, non-cash tax movements, and one-time events.\n* **Non-GAAP Adjusted EPS:** Management defines Adjusted EPS by starting with GAAP net earnings (loss) and adjusting for the per-share impact (net of tax effects) of items that management believes: (1) may affect trends in underlying performance from year to year, and (2) are not considered normal, recurring cash operating expenses [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.98].\n  * **Specific items excluded in the calculation:** Impairment of non-current assets, deferred income tax expense (or recovery), realized and unrealized gains/losses from investments in derivatives and marketable securities, loss on divestiture of mining interests, share-based payments, standby costs, abnormal costs, restructuring costs, write-downs on assets held-for-sale, and write-downs of mineral inventory [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.88, FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.98].\n\n---\n\n### 2. Q3 Reconciliation (GAAP vs. Non-GAAP)\n\nThe following table shows how the calculation methods bridge GAAP Net Loss to Non-GAAP Adjusted Net Loss and EPS for Q3 2024 vs. Q3 2023 (in thousands of dollars, except share and per-share amounts) [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.4, p.98]:\n\n| Metric / Adjustment Item | Q3 2024 (Three Months Ended Sep 30, 2024) | Q3 2023 (Three Months Ended Sep 30, 2023) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Net loss as reported (GAAP)** | **($26,593)** | **($27,149)** |\n| *Adjustments for non-cash or unusual items:* | | |\n| Deferred income tax expense | $13,235 | $3,729 |\n| (Gain) loss from investment in marketable securities | ($108) | $685 |\n| Loss on divestiture of mining interest | \u2014 | $1,646 |\n| Share-based payments | $2,935 | $2,762 |\n| Restructuring costs | \u2014 | $6,428 |\n| Write-down of mineral inventory | \u2014 | $972 |\n| **Adjusted net loss (Non-GAAP)** | **($10,531)** | **($10,927)** |\n| Weighted average shares on issue \u2013 basic | 301,000,656 | 286,921,720 |\n| **GAAP Basic / Diluted EPS** | **($0.09)** | **($0.09)** |\n| **Non-GAAP Adjusted EPS** | **($0.03)** | **($0.04)** |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Impact on Q3 Mining EPS Interpretation\n\n1. **Magnitude of Losses vs. Core Cash Operations:**\n   Looking strictly at GAAP EPS of **($0.09)** in Q3 2024 suggests a heavy quarterly loss of $26.6 million [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.4, p.98]. However, the Non-GAAP calculation reveals that **60.4% ($16.06 million)** of the Q3 2024 GAAP net loss came from non-cash items\u2014predominantly a $13.24 million non-cash deferred income tax expense and $2.94 million in share-based payments [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.98]. Removing these items shows an Adjusted Net Loss of $10.5 million, or **($0.03)** per share, indicating that core mining operations were much closer to breakeven than the headline GAAP figure implies [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.55, p.98].\n\n2. **Year-over-Year Operational Trend vs. Flat Headline EPS:**\n   Under GAAP, basic and diluted EPS appeared completely flat year-over-year at **($0.09)** in both Q3 2024 and Q3 2023 [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.4]. Under the Non-GAAP method, Adjusted EPS improved from **($0.04)** in Q3 2023 to **($0.03)** in Q3 2024 [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.55, p.98]. \n   * Furthermore, understanding *which* non-GAAP adjustments occurred in each period clarifies the operational shift: Q3 2023 GAAP losses were driven by operational wind-downs and one-off items ($6.43 million in restructuring costs, $1.65 million loss on divestiture of mining interest, and $0.97 million in inventory write-downs), whereas Q3 2024 had none of these operational charges (and mine operating earnings actually rose 119% from $13.0 million to $28.5 million), but its GAAP bottom line was instead masked by a much higher non-cash deferred tax charge ($13.24 million vs. $3.73 million) [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.70, p.98].\n\n3. **Decision-Making and Comparability Limitations:**\n   Management uses Adjusted EPS for forecasting, operational and strategic decision-making, evaluating management performance, and calculating financial covenants because stripping out non-recurring and non-cash items improves period-to-period comparability of the underlying mines [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.88]. However, because Non-GAAP measures lack standardized definitions under IFRS, the company's specific exclusion choices (such as removing share-based payments and deferred taxes) may differ from peer mining companies, meaning Adjusted EPS cannot be used in isolation as a substitute for GAAP EPS [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.88, 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.85].", "expectedOutput": "Non-GAAP and GAAP definitions and calculation methods affect Q3 mining EPS interpretation because non-GAAP measures, like Adjusted EPS, are calculated using company-specific methods that may differ from other companies and lack standardized definitions. This can make comparing EPS across companies challenging. For Q3, First Majestic Silver Corp. reports both a basic loss per share (EPS) of ($0.09) and an adjusted EPS of ($0.03), with the adjusted figure reflecting certain company adjustments. Refer to pages 42-52 for details and reconciliations between non-GAAP and GAAP measures.", "context": [".3 | $6.4 | NM | $6.4 | NM | $38.6 | ($12.9) | NM |\n| **Shareholders** | | | | | | | | |\n| Loss per Share (\"EPS\") - Basic | ($0.09) | ($0.17) | 47% | ($0.09) | 0% | ($0.30) | ($0.52) | 42% |\n| Adjusted EPS(1) | ($0.03) | ($0.07) | 57% | ($0.04) | 21% | ($0.17) | ($0.06) | NM |\n\nThese measures do not have standardized meaning under the Company's financial reporting framework and the methods used by the Company calculate these measures may differ from methods used by Other companies with similar descriptions See \"Non-GAAP Measures on pages 42 to 52 for further details on these measures and reconciliati