 its debt, which limits cash flow available for other needs [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.78]. While the reports do not provide a combined quantitative stress-test model for solvency as a whole, a simultaneous 10% unhedged drop in silver prices (reducing immediate inventory values and operating cash inflows) alongside uninsured political disruptions to production would directly squeeze the operating cash flows relied upon to service debt and meet financial obligations as they arise [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.73; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.78; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.114].", "expectedOutput": "If severe political upheaval occurred alongside a 10% drop in silver prices, First Majestic's mining solvency could be significantly threatened. The political risks\u2014including expropriation, tax increases, or foreign exchange controls, as outlined in the context\u2014could disrupt operations and financial stability. A 10% decline in silver prices would reduce net earnings by approximately $1.56 million (per the sensitivity table), further weakening financial results. Without political risk insurance and with revenues directly tied to volatile commodity prices, the company's ability to meet its obligations and continue operations could be compromised if both events occurred simultaneously.", "context": ["| | Cash and cash equivalents | Restricted cash | Value added taxes receivable | Other financial assets | Trade and other payables | Net assets (liabilities) exposure | Effect of +/- 10% change in currency |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Canadian Dollar | $1,097 | $\u2014 | $\u2014 | $1,282 | ($5,422) | ($1,878) | ($188) |\n| Mexican Peso | 25,748 | 86,726 | 44,614 | \u2014 | (63,988) | 93,100 | 9,310 |\n| | $26,845 | $86,726 | $44,614 | $1,282 | ($69,410) | $91,222 | $9,122 |\n\nUncertainty in the Estimation of Mineral Resources and Mineral Reserves, and Metal Recoveries\n\nAs at December 31, 2024, the Company's exposure to interest rate risk on interest bearing liabilities is limited to its debt facilitie