No title, legend, or axis labels appear in the image.\\n\\nGRUPO MEXICO\\n]", "input": "Evaluate investor interpretation challenges when presenting GRUPO MEXICO's color-coded data lacking titles, labels, and legend.", "actualOutput": "Based on the visual data and figures presented in Grupo M\u00e9xico\u2019s 2024 Sustainable Development Report (*Informe de Desarrollo Sustentable*), there are multiple instances where color-coded charts (primarily donut/ring charts) are presented without titles, category labels, or legends, or with cropped/incomplete text. \n\nEvaluating these specific disclosures highlights several key interpretation challenges for investors and analysts:\n\n### 1. Inability to Identify Categories Behind Color Segments\nWhen charts rely solely on color-coding without a legend or direct segment labels, investors cannot determine what the underlying percentages represent without cross-referencing or guessing from surrounding text:\n* **Unlabeled Supplier & Entity Breakdowns:** In the *Valor Compartido* (Shared Value) section, a standalone donut chart displays three segments\u2014**Orange (77.6%)**, **Black (11.7%)**, and **Gray (10.7%)**\u2014accompanied only by the note *\"Montos en USD millones\"* (\"Amounts in USD millions\"), lacking any title, legend, or axis labels [IDS24_ESP_03_Valor_Compartido_ebook p.35]. Similarly, two comparative donut charts for **Southern Copper Corporation (US$ 2,531.3)** (orange 75.9%, black 14.6%, gray 9.5%) and **Divisi\u00f3n Infraestructura (US$ 265.5)** (orange 52.4%, black 40.5%, gray 7.0%) split data into three unlabeled segments with no legend, failing to state within the graphic what the segments represent or what the dollar figures measure (only in a separate multi-chart view is the color key revealed as *Nacional*, *Locales*, and *Extranjero*, where the legend itself is partially cut off at the right edge) [IDS24_ESP_03_Valor_Compartido_ebook p.35].\n* **Isolated Social Metrics:** In the *Social* section under *Infraestructura*, a ring chart presents **Teal/blue (53%)**, **Gray (45%)**, and **Black (2%)** with no title, legend, or axis labels, and with surrounding text cut off (\"era\"), making standalone interpretation impossible [IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.15].\n\n### 2. Ambiguity in Comparative and Demographic Analysis\nWhen evaluating workforce demographics across divisions (*Diversidad e inclusi\u00f3n*), inconsistent or missing labels force investors to infer missing categories by comparing adjacent charts:\n* **Missing and Cropped Age Group Labels:** In the workforce age distribution charts, one donut chart displays **Teal (64.2%)**, **Gray (19.3%)**, and **Black (16.4%)**, where only the black segment is labeled (*\"> 50 a\u00f1os\"*). Because the chart has no title, legend, or labels for the teal and gray segments, their categories are not directly identified [IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.58].\n* In adjacent comparative charts for *Divisi\u00f3n Infraestructura* and *Corporativo GM*, labels for the largest blue segments (**64.4%** and **59.5%**) are either missing or cut off at the edge (showing only partial text like *\"...os\"* or *\"< 30...\"*), requiring the reader to deduce that the unlabeled blue segment corresponds to the *30\u201350 a\u00f1os* age bracket shown in other intact charts [IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.57, IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.58]. Furthermore, these charts do not state the total headcount (the denominator) on which the percentages are based [IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.58].\n\n### 3. Unexplained Unaccounted Portions (Percentages Not Summing to 100%)\nIn the *Ambiental* (Environmental) section, color-coded donut charts suffer from both missing/cropped labels and incomplete percentage totals, creating reconciliation challenges for ESG analysts:\n* **Incomplete Totals and Unlabeled Segments:** One three-segment donut chart displays **Green (58%)** with partially cropped text (*\"...de combustibles ...erg\u00eda\"*), **Beige (21%)** with no visible label at all, and **Dark gray (13%)** labeled *\"Bienes arrendados\"* (leased assets) [IDS24_ESP_06_Ambiental_ebook p.63]. Not only is the 21% beige category unidentifiable, but the three segments sum to only **92%**, leaving 8% of the metric unaccounted for [IDS24_ESP_06_Ambiental_ebook p.63].\n* A second donut chart on the same page shows **Olive green (45%)**, **Light beige/gray (28%)**, and **Dark gray (16%)**, with a single label at the lower right reading *\"Uso de combustible\"* (\"Fuel use\") [IDS24_ESP_06_Ambiental_ebook p.63]. Here, individual segment labels/legends are absent, and the segments sum to **89%**, leaving the remaining 11% unrepresented and unexplained [IDS24_ESP_06_Ambiental_ebook p.63].\n\n### 4. Separation of Visuals from Decorative Illustrations\nThroughout the sustainability report, Grupo M\u00e9xico frequently uses flat-style illustrations showing characters pointing at bar charts, line graphs, and color-coded donut charts that contain no text, numbers, labels, or legends [IDS24_ESP_02_Nuestro_Enfoque_ebook p.16, IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.11, IDS24_ESP_06_Ambiental_ebook p.20]. While these are purely decorative graphics meant to convey themes of analytics or sustainability, the lack of titles and legends on *actual* data charts elsewhere in the report blurs the line between decorative graphics and substantive disclosures, increasing the cognitive load required for investors to extract verifiable ESG and financial data.", "expectedOutput": "Investors may struggle to interpret GRUPO MEXICO's data, as the absence of a title, labels, and legend makes it unclear what the represented percentages (39%, 34%, 27%) and colors (orange, green, blue) refer to. This lack of context can lead to confusion and incorrect conclusions about the data's meaning.", "context": [" (39%), and blue (27%). The percentages sum to 100%. No title, legend, or axis labels appear in the image.\n\nGRUPO MEXICO\n"], "retrievalContext": ["controles preventivos y 