EMENTS\\n\\nInterest Rate Risk\\n\\nThe sensitivity of the Company's net earnings or loss and comprehensive income or loss due to changes in the exchange rates of the Canadian dollar and the Mexican peso against the U.S. dollar is included in the table below:\\n\\nThe following table summarizes the Company's exposure to commodity price risk and their impact on net earnings:\\n\\n<invoke name=\"noop\">\\n</invoke>\\n\\nPage 62\\n\\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of the audited consolidated financial statements\\n\\nThe Company is exposed to interest rate risk on its short-term investments debt facilities and lease liabilities. The Company monitors its exposure to interest rates and has not entered into any derivative contracts to manage this risk. The Company's interest-bearing financial assets comprise of cash and cash equivalents which bear interest at a mixture of variable and fixed rates for pre-set periods of time\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Annual Report\\n\\n26 FINANCIAL INSTRUMENTS AND RELATED RISK MANAGEMENT (continued)\\n\\n(Tabular amounts are expressed in thousands of US dollars\\n]", "input": "How do First Majestic\u2019s approaches to CAD/MXN fx risk compare to commodity and interest rate risk?", "actualOutput": "First Majestic Silver Corp. takes distinct approaches to managing its foreign exchange (CAD/MXN), commodity price, and interest rate risks, differing primarily in its willingness to use derivative hedging instruments:\n\n### 1. Foreign Exchange Risk (CAD and MXN) \u2014 *Occasional Short-Term Derivative Hedging*\n* **Exposure:** First Majestic is exposed to foreign exchange risk primarily through financial instruments denominated in Canadian dollars (CAD) and Mexican pesos (MXN), which directly impact net earnings or loss [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.113]. Specifically, this exposure spans cash and cash equivalents, restricted cash, value-added taxes (VAT) receivable, other financial assets, and trade and other payables [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.114].\n* **Management Approach:** Unlike its approach to commodity and interest rate risks, First Majestic actively permits hedging for currency risk. To manage foreign exchange risk and hedge its cash flows, the company **may occasionally enter into short-term foreign currency derivatives, such as forwards and options**, and from time to time utilizes derivatives specifically to manage exposures to the Mexican Peso [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.113]. \n* **2024 Activity & Sensitivity:** During Q2 2024, the company recorded an unrealized gain of $0.1 million on fair value adjustments to its foreign currency derivatives [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.41], though for the full year ended December 31, 2024, unrealized gains were $nil and the company held no open foreign currency derivatives at year-end (nor at the end of Q1, Q2, or Q3 2024) [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.40; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.41; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.42; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69]. As of December 31, 2024, net exposure stood at $(1,878) thousand in CAD and $93,100 thousand in MXN (totaling $91,222 thousand), where a +/- 10% currency change against the USD would impact net earnings by $(188) thousand for CAD and $9,310 thousand for MXN (a total net impact of $9,122 thousand) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.114].\n\n---\n\n### 2. Commodity Price Risk (Silver and Gold) \u2014 *Unhedged / No Long-Term Derivatives*\n* **Exposure:** The company faces direct and immediate commodity price risk on silver and gold, which affects the value of its related financial instruments, non-financial items (such as metals in dor\u00e9 inventory), and net earnings [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.40; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69]. Management notes that revenues are directly dependent on volatile commodity prices beyond the company's control [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69].\n* **Management Approach:** In contrast to its occasional use of short-term FX derivatives, First Majestic **does not use long-term derivative instruments to hedge its commodity price risk to silver or gold** [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69].\n* **2024 Sensitivity:** Exposure is measured on metals held in inventory. As of December 31, 2024, a +/- 10% change in metal prices on inventory would impact net earnings by $1,557 thousand for silver and $676 thousand for gold, for a total impact of $2,233 thousand [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114].\n\n---\n\n### 3. Interest Rate Risk \u2014 *Monitoring Only / No Derivatives Used*\n* **Exposure:** First Majestic is exposed to interest rate risk on its short-term investments, debt facilities, and lease liabilities [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114]. Its interest-bearing financial assets consist of cash and cash equivalents that bear interest at a mix of variable and fixed rates for pre-set periods, while its interest-bearing liabilities are limited to its debt facilities and lease liabilities [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114].\n* **Management Approach:** Rather than using derivatives as it occasionally does for FX risk, the company simply **monitors its exposure to interest rates and has not entered into any derivative contracts to manage this risk** [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.114].\n* **2024 Sensitivity:** Because of the structure of its interest-bearing assets and liabilities, sensitivity is minimal; as of December 31, 2024 (and throughout all quarters of 2024), a 25-basis-point increase or decrease in market interest rates does not have a significant impact on the company's net earnings or loss [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.40; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.42; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114].\n\n---\n\n### Summary Comparison\n\n| Risk Category | Primary Sources of Exposure | Hedging / Derivative Policy | Sensitivity Metric (as of Dec 31, 2024) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Foreign Exchange (CAD/MXN)** | Financial instruments in CAD and MXN (cash, restricted cash, VAT receivable, other financial assets, payables) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114] | **Occasionally uses short-term derivatives** (forwards and options) to hedge cash flows / MXN exposure [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69] | **+/- 10% FX rate change vs. USD:** $(188)k for CAD; $9,310k for MXN ($9,122k net) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.114] |\n| **Commodity Price (Silver/Gold)** | Revenues and metals in dor\u00e9 inventory [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.40; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69] | **Does not use long-term derivatives** to hedge silver or gold price risk [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69] | **+/- 10% metal price change (inventory):** $1,557k for silver; $676k for gold ($2,233k total) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114] |\n| **Interest Rate** | Short-term investments (cash/equivalents at mixed fixed/variable rates), debt facilities, and lease liabilities [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114] | **Monitors exposure only;** has not entered into any derivative contracts [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.114] | **+/- 25 bps market rate change:** No significant impact on net earnings or loss [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.69, p.114] |", "expectedOutput": "First Majestic manages CAD/MXN foreign exchange (fx) risk by occasionally using short-term derivatives, such as forwards and options, to hedge cash flows. In contrast, for commodity price risk (silver and gold), the Company does not use long-term derivatives to hedge and is fully exposed to price volatility. For interest rate risk, exposure is limited to debt facilities and lease liabilities, and the Company has not entered into derivative contracts to manage this risk, monitoring it instead. Thus, active hedging is used only for some fx exposures, while commodity and interest rate risks are not hedged with derivatives.", "context": ["The Company is exposed to foreign exchange risk primarily relating to financial instruments that are denominated in Canadian dollars or Mexican pesos, which would impact the Company's net earnings or loss_ To manage foreign exchange risk, the Company may occasionally enter into short-term foreign currency derivatives, such as forwards and options, to hedge its cash flows.\n\n(c) Financial risk management (continued)\n\nCommodity Price Risk\n\nFrom time to time, the Company utilizes certain derivatives to manage its foreign exchange exposures to the Mexic