ion in the year was $124.0 million, representing a 1% decrease compared to 5124.7 million in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to:\\n\\nImpairment decreased by $125.2 million compared to the same period of the previous year. In March 2023, the Company recorded 5125.2 million  impairment in respect of its Jerritt Canyon mine following the temporary suspension of mining operations.\\n\\nage 29\\n\\nGeneral and administrative expense in the year was $39.6 million, representing a 2% increase compared to $38 million in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to higher due diligence costs incurred as a result of the Gatos acquisition which completed on January 16, 2025 and higher legal and professional fees related to the Company's ongoing NAFTA proceedings. This was offset by higher severance costs incurred in 2023 related to restructuring efforts to optimize the Company's workforce:.\\n\\nCost of sales in the year were $344.7 million, representing a 16% decrease compared to $410.1 in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to:\\n\\nRestructuring costs decreased by $6.9 million compared to the same period in the previous year. In 2023, restructuring costs were related to the Company optimizing its workforce primarily at San Dimas, as well as at the Durango regional office and Santa Elena_\\n\\n$12.1 million decrease related to lower depletion at Jerritt Canyon due to the temporary suspension of mining activities-\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Annual Report\\n\\n513.4 million decrease in one-time standby costs primarily related to one-time severance and demobilization costs following the temporary suspension of mining activities at Jerritt Canyon in 2023; and\\n\\nWrite down on asset held-for-sale decrease by $7.2 million compared to the same period of the previous year. In 2023, the Company recorded a write-down related to La Parrilla based on the change in value of shares of Silver Storm Mining (formerly Golden Tag Resources) at the end of the reporting period.\\n\\n10. Investment and other income for the year totaled $5.4 million, representing a 41% decrease compared to $9.2 million in the same period of the previous year. The decrease in the investment and other income was primarily due to an\\n\\na $1.9 million increase in environmental duties and royalties due to increased production at Ermita\\xf1o which resulted in increased royalties paid;\\n]", "input": "If Santa Elena\u2019s production surged as Jerritt Canyon stayed suspended, how would overall costs and depreciation shift?", "actualOutput": "Based on First Majestic Silver Corp.\u2019s 2024 financial reports (which reflect this exact scenario\u2014record surging production at Santa Elena alongside the continued temporary suspension of Jerritt Canyon since March 2023), overall costs and depletion/depreciation shift in the following ways:\n\n### 1. Shifts in Overall Costs\n* **Significant Drop in Consolidated Operating & Standby Costs (Jerritt Canyon Impact):** Keeping Jerritt Canyon suspended eliminates its active mining, milling, and indirect production costs, driving a sharp reduction in overall labour, consumables, energy, insurance, services, selling, and maintenance costs (which fell by $73.9 million in 2024, alongside a $13.4 million reduction in one-time standby/severance costs) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.106, p.130]. \n* **Shift to Ongoing Holding Costs (Jerritt Canyon Impact):** Instead of active production costs, Jerritt Canyon generates ongoing care-and-maintenance holding costs (roughly $4.1 million to $4.7 million per quarter in 2024) for water management and treatment, environmental permits, and plant maintenance [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.63; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.67; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.66; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.106].\n* **Higher Volume-Linked & Royalty Costs at Santa Elena:** A production surge at Santa Elena increases total variable costs at the mine, specifically driving higher **environmental duties and royalty costs** (owing to increased production from the Ermita\u00f1o underground mine and higher sales) [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.73; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.93, p.130] as well as higher **workers' participation (profit-sharing)** allocated to Santa Elena due to its strong performance [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.60].\n* **Lower Per-Ounce Unit Costs (Cash Costs & AISC):** On a per-unit basis, surging throughput and silver/gold production at Santa Elena dilute fixed costs and lower both Santa Elena's and the company's consolidated **Cash Cost per AgEq ounce** and **All-In Sustaining Cost (AISC) per AgEq ounce**, helping offset cost inflation and lower production at other mines [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.54, p.59; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.93, p.94].\n\n---\n\n### 2. Shifts in Depletion, Depreciation, and Amortization (DD&A)\nBecause mining properties are depleted on a units-of-production basis while certain plant and equipment are depreciated over time, the two mines pull consolidated DD&A in opposite directions:\n* **Depletion & Depreciation Surge at Santa Elena:** Because depletion is tied directly to tonnes/ounces produced, surging production at Santa Elena\u2014combined with higher fixed asset depreciation at the site\u2014significantly increases DD&A from Mexican operations (driving an $11.5 million increase for the full year 2024, and a $5.7 million increase in Q4 2024 alone) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.106, p.109]. Specifically, depletion and amortization of Santa Elena\u2019s mining interests rose from $18.7 million in 2023 to $23.9 million in 2024 [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.44].\n* **Depletion Drops to Zero & Depreciation Falls at Jerritt Canyon:** With mining activities suspended, depletion of Jerritt Canyon's mineral properties drops to **$0** (compared to $6.65 million in 2023) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.44], while property, plant, and equipment depreciation decreases substantially [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.25]. Across 2024, the suspension of Jerritt Canyon resulted in a $12.1 million decrease in DD&A [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.106].\n* **Net Consolidated DD&A Effect:** In total, the surge in depletion and fixed-asset depreciation at Santa Elena nearly offsets the steep drop in depletion/depreciation at the suspended Jerritt Canyon mine (in 2024, the $11.5 million Mexican operations increase and $12.1 million Jerritt Canyon decrease resulted in consolidated DD&A remaining virtually flat at $124.0 million vs. $124.7 million in 2023\u2014and trending higher by 17% in Q4 as Santa Elena's produced tonnes climbed after Jerritt Canyon's depletion had already zeroed out) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.106, p.109].", "expectedOutput": "With increased production at Santa Elena and the suspension of operations at Jerritt Canyon, overall costs would shift in the following ways:\n\n- **Cost reduction overall**: Cost of sales decreased by 16% ($344.7 million vs. $410.1 million) primarily due to Jerritt Canyon's suspension, which led to lower labor, consumables, energy, and one-time standby costs.\n- **Santa Elena-specific cost increases**: Depletion and depreciation from Mexican operations rose by $11.5 million, driven largely by higher production and increased fixed asset depreciation at Santa Elena.\n- **Depreciation/depletion overall**: Total depletion, depreciation, and amortization actually decreased by 1% ($124.0 million vs. $124.7 million), as the reduced depletion at Jerritt Canyon (down $12.1 million) outweighed the increases at Santa Elena.\n\nIn summary, while Santa Elena's increased output raised its own depreciation and associated costs, the temporary shutdown of Jerritt Canyon led to decreased overall costs and slightly reduced total depreciation and depletion.", "context": ["Mine holding costs in the year were $23.7 million, representing a 7% increase compared to $22.1 million in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to the temporary suspension of Jerritt Canyon in Q1 2023 and care and maintenance activities for the Santa Elena underground mine. This was partially offset by lower holding costs due to the sale of La Guitarra and La Parrilla in the first and third quarters of 2023, respectively.\n\nan $11.5 million increase in depletion and depreciation from the Mexican operations primarily due to an increase in production at Santa Elena as well as higher fixed asset depreciation at Santa Elena and La Encantada.\n\nLoss on sale of mining interest decrease by $3.0 million compared to the same period of the previous year. This was due to the sale of La Guitarra to Sierra Madre Gold and Silver Ltd. on March 29, 2023. Based on the carrying value of the asset at the time of disposal of $34.3 million, and the working capital adjustment of $0.2 million, the Company had recorded a loss on disposition of $1.4 million in the first quarter of the prior year .\n\n$1.8 million increase in abnormal costs at San Dimas during the second quarter as the Company had to use higher cost diesel from back up energy sources due to low water levels at the Company's Las Truchas hydroelectric dam and damage to the power lines from a wildfire adjacent to the hydroelectric plant; and\n\nS1.7 million increase in worker participation costs in Mexico_\n\na weaker Mexican peso relative to the US dollar, which averaged 3% weaker compared to 2023_\n\na $73.9 million decrease in labour, consumables and energy costs during the year along with a decrease in other costs including insurance, services, selling and maintenance costs. This was primarily due to the temporary suspension of operations at the Jerritt Canyon Mine during Q1 of 2023, a reduced workforce in Jerritt Canyon and across the organization, along with lower production at San Dimas and La Encantada during the year;\n\nDepletion, depreciation and amortization in the year was $124.0 million, representing a 1% decrease compared to 5124.7 million in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to:\n\nImpairment decreased by $125.2 million compared to the same period of the previous year. In March 2023, the Company recorded 5125.2 million  impairment in respect of its Jerritt Canyon mine following the temporary suspension of mining operations.\n\nage 29\n\nGeneral and administrative expense in the year was $39.6 million, representing a 2% increase compared to $38 million in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to higher due diligence costs incurred as a result of the Gatos acquisition which completed on January 16, 2025 and higher legal and professional fees related to the Company's ongoing NAFTA proceedings. This was offset by higher severance costs incurred in 2023 related to restructuring efforts to optimize the Company's workforce:.\n\nCost of sales in the year were $344.7 million, representing a 16% decrease compared to $410.1 in the same period of the previous year, primarily attributed to:\n\nRestructuring costs decreased by $6.9 million compared to the same period in the previous year. In 2023, restructuring costs were related to the Company optimizing its workforce primarily at San Dimas, as well as at the Durango regional office and Santa Elena_\n\n$12.1 million decrease related to lower depletion at Jerritt Canyon due to the temporary suspension of mining activities-\n\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Annual Report\n\n513.4 million decrease in one-time standby costs primarily related to one-time severance and demobilization costs following the temporary suspension of mining activities at Jerritt Canyon in 2023; and\n\nWrite down on asset held-for-sale decrease by $7.2 million compared to the same period of the previous year. In 2023, the Company recorded a write-down related to La Parrilla based on the change in value of shares of Silver Storm Mining (formerly Golden Tag Resources) at the end of the reporting period.\n\n10. Investment and other income for the year totaled $5.4 million, representing a 41% decrease compared to $9.2 million in the same period of the previous year. The decrease in the investment and other income was primarily due to an\n\na $1.9 million increase in environmental duties and royalties due to increased production at Ermita\u00f1o which resulted in increased royalties paid;\n"], "retrievalContext": ["Page\n\nThe Jerritt Canyon Gold Mine is an underground mining complex located in northern Nevada, United States. Jerritt Canyon was discovered in 1972 and has been in production since 1981 having produced over 9.5 million ounces of gold over its 40- year production history: The operation, which was purchased by the Company on April 30, 2021, has one of only three permitted gold processing plants in Nevada that uses roasting in its treatment of ore. This processing plant has a capacity of 4,000 tpd.\n\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 First Quarter Report\n\nOn March 20, 2023, the Company temporarily suspended mining activities at Jerritt Canyon to reduce overall costs and refocus mining and exploration plans at the mine. The property consists of a large, underexplored land package consisting of 30,821 hectares (119 square miles) . Jerritt Canyon is 100% owned by the Company-\n\nJerritt Canyon Gold Mine, Nevada, United States", "Page 18\n\nThe Jerritt Canyon Gold Mine is an underground mining complex located in northern Nevada, United States. Jerritt Canyon was discovered in 1972 and has been in production since 1981 having produced over 9.5 million ounces of gold over its 40- year production history: The operation, which was purchased by the Company on April 30, 2021, has one of only three permitted gold processing plants in Nevada that uses roasting in its treatment of ore. This processing plant has a capacity of 4,000 tpd.\n\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Third Quarter Report\n\nOn March 20, 2023, the Company temporarily suspended mining activities at Jerritt Canyon to reduce overall costs and refocus mining and exploration plans at the mine. The property consists of a large, under-explored land package consisting of 30,821 hectares (119 square miles). Jerritt Canyon is 100% owned by the Company.\n\nJerritt Canyon Gold Mine, Nevada, United States", "First Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Second Quarter Reporu\n\nOn March 20, 2023, the Company temporarily suspended mining activities at Jerritt Canyon to reduce overall costs and refocus mining and exploration plans at the mine The property consists of a large, underexplored land package consisting of 30,821 hectares (119 square miles) . Jerritt Canyon is 100% owned by the Company-\n\nJerritt Canyon Gold Mine, Nevada, United States", "Page 24\n\nThe Jerritt Canyon Gold Mine is an underground mining complex located in northern Nevada, United States. Jerritt Canyon was discovered in 1972 and has been in production since 1981 having produced over 9.5 million ounces of gold over its 40- year production history: The operation, which was purchased by the Company on April 30, 2021, has one of only three permitted gold processing plants in Nevada that uses roasting in its treatment of ore. This processing plant has a capacity of 4,000 tpd.\n\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Annual Report", "During the quarter, the Company incurred $4.4 million in holding costs at Jerritt Canyon, primarily for care and maintenance activities such as water management and treatment, maintaining environmental permits and controls, keeping the plant well-maintained for future processing and maintaining land access. The Company continues to focus on optimizing holding costs and completing technical studies that will support an eventual restart of the operation in the future_\n\nJerritt Canyon\n\nOperations at the Jerritt Canyon mine were placed on temporary suspension in March 2023. As of April 24 , 2023, all activities at the Jerritt Canyon processing plant were fully suspended. In-circuit recovery efforts performed in the first quarter of 2024 resulted in production of 647 ounces of gold.", "During the quarter, the Company incurred 54.1 million in holding costs at Jerritt Canyon, primarily for care and maintenance activities such as water management and treatment; maintaining environmental permits and controls, keeping the plant well-maintained for future processing and maintaining land access. The Company continues to focus on optimizing holding costs and completing technical studies that will support an eventual restart of the operation in the future.\n\nJerritt Canyon\n\nOperations at the Jerritt Canyon mine were placed on temporary suspension in March 2023. As of April 24, 2023, all activities at the Jerritt Canyon processing plant were fully suspended. In-circuit recovery efforts performed in the second quarter of 2024 resulted in production of 74 ounces of gold, compared to 647 ounces of gold in the previous quarter .", "Page 20\n\nThe Jerritt Canyon Gold Mine is an underground mining complex located in northern Nevada, United States. Jerritt Canyon was discovered in 1972 and has been in production since 1981 having produced over 9.5 million ounces of gold over its 40- year production history: The operation, which was purchased by the Company on April 30, 2021, has one of only three permitted gold processing plants in Nevada that uses roasting in its treatment of ore. This processing plant has a capacity of 4,000 tpd.\n\nThe 2024 exploration program at Jerritt Canyon is planned to commence in July 2024, with approximately 25,000 metres of drilling planned for 2024.\n\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Second Quarter Reporu", "During the quarter, the Company incurred $4.7 million in holding costs at Jerritt Canyon; primarily for care and maintenance activities such as water management and treatment, maintaining environmental permits and controls, keeping the plant well-maintained for future processing and maintaining land access. The Company continues to focus on optimizing holding costs and completing technical studies that will support an eventual restart of the mine in the future.\n\nJerritt Canyon\n\nOperations at the Jerritt Canyon mine were placed on temporary suspension in March 2023_ As of April 24, 2023, all activities at the Jerritt Canyon processing plant were fully suspended. In-circuit recovery efforts performed in the third quarter of 2024 resulted in the recovery of 1,684 ounces of gold, compared to 74 ounces of gold in the previous quarter.", "Santa Elena's robust production continues: Production during the second quarter at Santa Elena totaled 2.6 million AgEq ounces, representing a 13% increase compared to Q1 and is on track to exceed the Company's original annual production guidance plan\n\nSecond Quarter Operational Highlights", "Santa Elena\n\nDuring the first quarter, Santa Elena produced 2,280,739 AgEq ounces consisting of 355,205 ounces of silver and 21,713 ounces of gold representing a decrease of 39% and 23%, respectively, when compared to the record production achieved in the prior quarter. Lower mined grades had been anticipated and are a consequence of stope scheduling in the mine plan. Silver and gold production is tracking above the Company's 2024 guidance production plan.\n\nSilver and gold recoveries averaged 69% and 95%, respectively, compared to 73% and 96% respectively; in the prior quarter. Reduced recoveries were expected and are a direct consequence of the lower mill feed head grades", "Santa Elena produced 2,685,375 AgEq ounces, 4% above the prior quarter, consisting of 376,203 ounces of silver and 27,435 ounces of gold.\n\nSanta Elena produced 2,685,375 AgEq ounces, 4% above the prior quarter, consisting of 376,203 ounces of silver and 27,435 ounces of eold\n\nSanta Elena\n\nThe mill processed a total of 259,919 tonnes of ore, slightly higher than the prior quarter with average silver and gold head grades relatively consistent at 68 g/t and 3.50 g/t, respectively.\n\nAISC per AgEq ounce tor the quarter was $14.38, representing a 5% decrease compared to $15.07 per AgEq ounce in the prior quarter. This was primarily attributable to the decrease in cash costs and lower profit sharing allocated to Santa Elena compared to the p